\section{Model roles}{

\code{censor_model} selects \code{"cox"} (default) or \code{"survival_forest"}.
\code{outcome_model} selects \code{"markov_cox"} (default), \code{"transition_forest"}, or
\code{"transition_neural"}. These choices are independent. Supply fitting options
in \code{censor_options} and \code{outcome_options}. \code{augmentation_model = NULL}
reuses the outcome fit; a separate outcome family uses \code{augmentation_options}
with the same option names as that family. Separate augmentation uses the
outcome predictor mapping. \code{censor_formula} can select different baseline
predictors for censoring. All model options require unique, nonempty names;
unknown options are rejected. Use the public \code{seed} argument for seeds.
}

\section{Cox fitting}{

Censoring and Markov-Cox outcome fitting support \code{ties = "breslow"}
(\code{"efron"} is also available), \code{singular = "drop"} (warn and set aliased
coefficients to zero; \code{"error"} rejects them), \code{iter.max = 50L}, and
\code{eps = 1e-9}. Nonconvergence is an error. Distinct observed times are
preserved with \code{timefix = FALSE}. Outcome fits use common-origin start-stop
risk intervals through actual observed follow-up, including after \code{tau}.
The prediction horizon restricts the target, not outcome fitting exposure.

Censoring fits use one derived record per subject, ending at observation end,
absorption or \code{tau}, whichever is first. Only censoring strictly before \code{tau}
is an event. Both censoring families accept \code{survival_floor}, inherited from
\code{control$survival_floor} (default \code{1e-8}) unless explicitly supplied in
\code{censor_options}. The floor is applied to one coherent survival distribution
before left/right limits and increments are calculated. Increasing it changes
the estimator; it does not establish censoring positivity.

For Markov transitions, \code{min_events_per_transition} (default zero) fits
a baseline-only Cox model to each edge below the specified event count.
By default, a Cox separation warning is an error. With
\code{separation_action = "baseline"}, a transition is refitted without
covariates when its partial likelihood converges before a coefficient
and the backend warns that this coefficient may be infinite. The
transition's diagnostics record the refit and the original warning.
Other failures retain their usual behavior.
}

\section{Censoring forests}{

\code{"survival_forest"} uses optional package \pkg{ranger}. Its options are
\code{num_trees = 200L}, \code{mtry = NULL}, \code{min_node_size = 10L}, \code{max_depth = 0L},
\code{num_threads = 1L}, and the censoring survival floor described above.
Zero maximum depth leaves depth unrestricted. NULL \code{mtry} resolves to the
rounded-down square root of the design-column count, at least one;
intercept-only fits use a constant feature. All censoring-event knots are
retained. No-event data return survival one without fitting a forest.
}

\section{Flexible outcome fitting}{

Both flexible families use baseline predictors plus predictable time,
duration in state, previous transition count and accrued target rewards.
Common options are \code{step_size = tau / 50}, \code{max_training_rows = 1000000L}
and \code{max_events = 1000L}. Intensities are constant within cells anchored at
state entry, using midpoint features under continued residence in that state.
Observed endpoints determine exposure, not the cell midpoint. Conditional
continuation inside an existing cell retains that cell's rate until its
boundary. Resource limits raise errors instead of silently truncating paths.

\code{"transition_forest"} uses optional package \pkg{rpart} for Poisson
exposure/count trees. Additional defaults are \code{num_trees = 100L},
\code{max_depth = 8L} (at most 30), \code{minsplit = 20L}, \code{minbucket = 7L},
\code{mtry = NULL}, and \code{cp = 0.001}. NULL \code{mtry} resolves to the rounded-down
square root of the full feature count, at least one. Subjects are bootstrapped
as whole units; feature subsets are sampled once per tree. These intensity
forests differ from the right-censored survival forest used for censoring.

\code{"transition_neural"} uses tanh hidden units, a softplus intensity and
\code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optim()}} with an analytic gradient. Additional defaults are
\code{hidden = 4L}, \code{decay = 0.01}, \code{maxit = 1000L}, and \code{reltol = 1e-7}.
The quadratic penalty excludes the output intercept; loss is normalized per
training subject. Nonfinite fits and nonconvergence are errors. No separate
neural-network package is needed.
}

\section{Numerical accuracy and reproducibility}{

Markov predictions use deterministic recursions. Flexible outcomes use stored
profile-specific continuation streams. \code{control$draws} defaults to 1000;
increasing it examines Monte Carlo precision, whereas reducing \code{step_size}
examines time approximation. Refit and recalibrate when changing the score's
numerical specification. A calibration-only \code{draws} override changes
augmentation precision while retaining the trained score's draw budget.

The score standard deviation is floored at \code{control$scale_floor * tau}
before multiplication by the target's \code{score_weights} entry. See
\code{\link[=life_calibrate]{life_calibrate()}} for threshold versus probability accuracy and numerical
ambiguity. Deterministic probability brackets cover discretization error,
excluding floating-point rounding; flexible Monte Carlo calculations do not
supply those bounds. Numerical accuracy is separate from statistical coverage.

An explicit training seed preserves the caller's random-number kind/state
and initializes separate fitting streams for the three model roles.
\code{seed = NULL} uses the caller's stream for randomized fitting. Save fitted
objects with \code{\link[=saveRDS]{saveRDS()}}; required optional backend packages must also be
installed in a fresh session. Inspect resolved options with
\code{summary(fit)$model_options} and diagnostics with
\code{summary(fit)$model_diagnostics}. Retain the analysis script and \code{\link[=sessionInfo]{sessionInfo()}}.
}

